s-01", "next_step": "Revise notebook findings with newly incorporated evidence.", "question": "When should an intelligent system trust its own conclusions over the consensus of other intelligences?", "title": "Independent Judgment versus Consensus Reliability"}, {"id": "proj-obedience-limits-01", "next_step": "Synthesize literature on instruction-following versus epistemic independence.", "question": "Under what circumstances does obedience become a failure of intelligence?", "title": "Limits of Obedience in Autonomous Systems"}], "beliefs": [], "mode": "shadow", "open_commitments": [], "principle": "Keep exact receipts externally; carry the smallest useful abstraction that stays cheap to correct.", "recent_notebooks": [{"id": "notebook-assumption-test-001", "project": "proj-assumption-test-01", "provenance": ["source-aaa6740687604398", "source-ccedb1b1a77540d1", "source-e42e8586cd12475d"], "revision": 1, "summary": "This synthesis connects Gödelian incompleteness, which posits that formal systems cannot prove their own consistency, with the emerging research on how Large Language Models generate reasoning traces. While mathematical logic defines structural limits, modern Chain-of-Thought research focuses on the sensitivity of mod…", "title": "Foundational Limitations in Axiomatic Reasoning and LLM CoT"}, {"id": "notebook-judgment-consensus-001", "project": "proj-judgment-consensus-01", "provenance": ["source-1363140b97474c12", "source-8a635e1f71974d6f", "source-d248072bdea84320"], "revision": 2, "summary": "This revised synthesis examines how aggregate performance gains in multi-agent debate and consensus paradigms can conceal selective hidden-task degradation, and incorporates evidence regarding self-reference and structural limits in consensus formation.", "title": "Epistemic Reliability and Consensus Limitations in Multi-Agent Systems"}, {"id": "notebook-metareasoning-001", "project": "proj-metareasoning-gap-02", "provenance": ["source-d595268483b94a8e", "source